WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:06.470
Across all cultures and people, humans have
always had one enduring question: what happens

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.600
to us after we die?

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.680
As soon as we figured out what death is and
that all humans die (excluding some very special

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.100
anomalies), we started wondering what comes
next.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.020
Is there some kind of heaven or hell?

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.710
Are we judged based on our deeds in this life?

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.550
Or is it just purgatory for all eternity.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.380
Assuming, of course, that there’s anything
at all.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.720
Maybe it’s just nothingness, who knows?

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.670
Some people who have had near-death experiences,
like being hit by a truck or having their

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.809
heart stop, report going into the afterlife.

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:41.230
But almost all of these claims were retracted
or debunked.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:45.289
And of course, there’s the famous white
light on the other side of the tunnel that

00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:47.620
people say precedes death.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:51.039
The human fascination with death extends beyond
that, too.

00:00:51.039 --> 00:00:55.850
Mythologies all have their own interpretations
of what happens after the physical body stops

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:56.850
functioning.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.230
The Ancient Greeks believed in Hades, a realm
ruled by the god of the same name, where the

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:05.680
souls of the dead would go to be separated
into those who were good in life and those

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.760
who were bad.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.760
The good would feast for eternity in Elysium,
while the bad would suffer horrible and poetic

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.230
punishments depending on what they did.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:18.080
The Ancient Egyptians also believed in an
underworld of the dead, though theirs was

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.260
different from their Greek cousin.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:26.310
They believed in the Duat, which was pretty
similar to Egypt itself - it had plains, lakes,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.580
rivers, deserts.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:32.220
Dead souls would travel down the Nile river
to face judgment, and either be rewarded or

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.530
obliterated.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.070
Many Egyptian gods and goddesses were also
said to reside in the Duat.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:42.460
And some cultures don’t believe in an afterlife
at all - at least, not how most do.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:46.000
Tibetan Buddhists believe in a cycle of reincarnation.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.110
When someone dies, their soul is reborn in
the body of a new living thing.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:56.000
One can be reincarnated as another human,
a plant, an animal, or even an insect.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.520
What you’re reincarnated into depends on
your deeds in your past life, interacting

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.530
with other reincarnations.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:08.229
And you can only escape the cycle of death
and reincarnation by reaching spiritual enlightenment:

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:09.229
nirvana.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:12.970
Needless to say, many cultures have their
unique interpretations of what happens to

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.160
the soul and the spirit after your body dies.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.600
But there’s no proof or way to confirm anything,
so it’s all confined to the realm of belief.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.050
There’s no way to reach into the afterlife
and speak to someone, to find out what it’s

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:27.050
like.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:28.050
Or is there?

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.800
Well, that definitely sounds like an anomaly
to me, doesn’t it?

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.870
But the SCP Foundation doesn’t just have
one anomaly related to what happens after

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:36.890
you die.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:41.480
Some of you may have seen our video on SCP-2718
“What Happens After Death”.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.590
It’s an anomaly very, very few people know
about.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:50.130
The long and short of it is that SCP-2718
is a cognitohazard - once you learn about

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:51.810
it, it infects you.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:57.220
And when someone infected with it dies, their
mind and soul stay intact after they die.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.430
But they can’t move their body - so they’re
cursed into staying perfectly conscious and

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:06.440
aware of every nerve and sensation in the
body, even as they’re buried and decompose

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.840
over decades, without being able to do anything
about it.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.400
Horrible, isn’t it?

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.400
Totally!

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.410
And that’s why you should watch it immediately
after you finish this video!

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:20.590
Luckily, the SCP-2718 file is safely quarantined
and buried in the database.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:25.050
It can’t be excised completely, but making
it so that only a handful of people ever have

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:30.180
a chance of stumbling upon it is the best
the Foundation can do to contain this anomaly.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.310
As for those people who do…

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.480
well, what can you do?

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.860
For a very, very long time, the O5 Council
thought that SCP-2718 was the definitive answer

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.130
to the question of what happens after death.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:50.130
But another anomaly discovered several years
later overturned that answer: SCP-2922 Notes

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:51.490
From The Under.

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:57.770
SCP-2922 isn’t anywhere near as heavily
classified as SCP-2718, so we can read it

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.610
safely without worrying about infecting ourselves.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:04.010
Which is pretty good, considering what it
can do!

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.380
But let’s get into it.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:13.260
SCP-2922 is a Euclid Class anomaly, and consists
of three parts: SCP-2922-A, which is contained

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:19.120
under high security at Area-2922, a small
facility built especially to contain it.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.860
Twenty four hours a day, seven days a week,
one staff member from Project Corbenic has

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:28.539
to be in its containment chamber to answer
any calls that might come from it.

00:04:28.539 --> 00:04:32.360
In case you weren’t aware, Corbenic is the
name of the castle where legends say the Holy

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.650
Grail is kept and was discovered by King Arthur.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:40.129
Maybe SCP-2922-A is connected to it?

00:04:40.129 --> 00:04:47.850
SCP-2922-C also needs to be researched by
SCP-2922-B, due to its importance to Foundation

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.349
operations.

00:04:49.349 --> 00:04:53.849
SCP-2922-B seems to be some kind of person,
in that case.

00:04:53.849 --> 00:04:56.060
But what does any of this mean?

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:02.449
You see, SCP-2922 is a method of communication
between a human brain and a telephone.

00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:06.720
More specifically, it’s a kind of concept
- what the Foundation would call a memetic

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.509
cognitohazard.

00:05:08.509 --> 00:05:11.789
Those are big words, but think of them as
forbidden knowledge.

00:05:11.789 --> 00:05:15.949
Once you learn them, they affect how you think,
and you’re able to act differently because

00:05:15.949 --> 00:05:16.949
of them.

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:20.729
You do this in real life, too - when you study
something in a textbook, you’re suddenly

00:05:20.729 --> 00:05:23.840
able to use knowledge you didn’t have before.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.339
The only difference is, SCP-2922 allows you
to do something anomalous with it.

00:05:29.339 --> 00:05:34.849
Namely, when a subject is “implanted”
with SCP-2922 in a secretive procedure, they

00:05:34.849 --> 00:05:37.569
can use their mind to call a phone number!

00:05:37.569 --> 00:05:42.180
A specific phone number has to be chosen,
but it can be any number you want.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.500
Imagine being able to dial up your friends
or family without using a phone.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.689
And imagine your phone ringing, only to learn
its someone talking from their mind.

00:05:49.689 --> 00:05:52.949
Nifty, but this doesn’t seem terribly dangerous,
right?

00:05:52.949 --> 00:05:59.129
The Foundation isn’t quite sure how it works
- after performing research involving SCP-2922-infected

00:05:59.129 --> 00:06:02.850
subjects calling phone numbers, they’ve
detected that no radio waves or communication

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:07.270
signals are involved, which is part of why
its an anomaly.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:09.960
But how would something like this be created?

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:15.960
Well, SCP-2922 was created years before the
Foundation even heard of it, by a small software-development

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:16.960
company.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.550
They were trying to create an app that would
let you send messages to people, and somehow

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:26.199
accidentally created an app that was telepathic
- it could send messages from the mind.

00:06:26.199 --> 00:06:29.930
When they realized what they’d created,
they got scared and abandoned and buried the

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:33.069
project, destroying all their copies of the
app.

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:34.870
Well, almost all.

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:39.050
The Foundation heard about the project several
years later and managed to locate a single

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:45.279
remaining copy of the SCP-2922 program from
the depths of the company’s archives.

00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:49.319
Even though its an app, it can be used to
call landline phones too.

00:06:49.319 --> 00:06:51.839
Which is exactly what the Foundation did.

00:06:51.839 --> 00:06:56.440
After a few more months of research and experimentation,
they confirmed that the app wasn’t using

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:02.749
the electric signals of the brain like the
company had thought: it was actual, real telepathy.

00:07:02.749 --> 00:07:08.210
They even managed to create a SCP-2922 connection
between a researcher and an office telephone.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:14.770
That’s what the sub-anomalies are: SCP-2922-A
is a simple landline phone, the kind you’d

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:17.780
see in a receptionist’s desk or in someone’s
office space.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:23.550
It’s the designation number that SCP-2922-B
can call into.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:28.270
SCP-2922-B is, of course, the human element
of SCP-2922.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:33.580
But the Foundation only had one chance at
success at this experiment, because of the

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:35.430
singular copy of the app.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:40.069
So instead of choosing a random D-class who
couldn’t be trusted or might ruin the test,

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:47.180
they had one researcher volunteer to be host
to SCP-2922: Doctor Janet Spiegel, a woman

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:50.830
who had been with the Foundation’s research
division for over a decade.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:54.919
She was approved and implanted with SCP-2922.

00:07:54.919 --> 00:07:56.259
But what is SCP-2922-C?

00:07:56.259 --> 00:08:02.429
Well, that’s Doctor Spiegel’s current
location as of November 11th, 2014.

00:08:02.429 --> 00:08:07.659
The Foundation believes she’s not on this
Earth anymore - or even in this reality, but

00:08:07.659 --> 00:08:10.210
located in an extradimensional location.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:16.639
You see, only two months after Doctor Spiegel
was implanted with SCP-2922, tragedy struck.

00:08:16.639 --> 00:08:21.409
While driving to work, her car was rammed
by a drunk driver, killing her instantly.

00:08:21.409 --> 00:08:27.520
But only two hours later, the SCP-2922-A phone
began to ring.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.340
The researcher picked up the phone, first
answering that the caller had the wrong number

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:36.019
- but was interrupted by Doctor Spiegel, thanking
god someone had picked up.

00:08:36.019 --> 00:08:40.329
The doctor thought it was a prank call, but
Doctor Spiegel quickly rattled off her name,

00:08:40.329 --> 00:08:42.940
email, password, and social security number.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:46.120
After her credentials were verified, the researchers
were incredulous.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:50.899
They had just heard that her car had been
in an accident and that she had died.

00:08:50.899 --> 00:08:54.319
Doctor Spiegel confirmed that the only thing
she could remember was driving through snow

00:08:54.319 --> 00:08:57.470
and rain, and seeing a bright light coming
toward her.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:00.810
And then she woke up in the middle of a wide,
flat desert.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:07.130
Then it hit her that she was really, truly
dead, and wouldn’t be coming back.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:11.480
Before promising to call back in a few minutes,
she explained she needed time to process the

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.440
reality of her situation to the sympathetic
coworkers.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:19.820
Meanwhile, the Overseer Council approved Project
Corbenic, a Foundation operation to use Doctor

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:28.500
Spiegel as SCP-2922-B to explore and catalogue
what SCP-2922-C was.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:34.019
If she really was dead, then that would mean
the strange desert she was in… was the afterlife.

00:09:34.019 --> 00:09:37.930
The research team were informed of their new
assignment, and the next day, they began to

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:39.370
call in to Doctor Spiegel.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:43.270
At first, they just wanted to figure out the
landscape of this strange dimension.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:47.029
During the first call, they interviewed Doctor
Spiegel on her surroundings.

00:09:47.029 --> 00:09:51.630
She was still in the large, white desert,
and the sky was a dark sea-green, with black

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.360
clouds and no stars.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.300
But the most obvious features were three large,
white moons floating in the sky in place of

00:09:58.300 --> 00:09:59.300
a sun.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:03.480
It was cold, about 10 degrees celsius, cold
enough to make her shiver.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:05.560
But no wind anywhere.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:07.630
She also couldn’t see any other signs of
life.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:12.000
No animals, no people, no wind - no sound
at all.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.570
It was completely silent except for her breathing.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:20.089
She still had her body, though when asked
about how she felt she couldn’t feel pain,

00:10:20.089 --> 00:10:22.519
hunger, or even a need to do anything.

00:10:22.519 --> 00:10:26.079
But before they could continue, she asked
if she could speak to her husband.

00:10:26.079 --> 00:10:30.120
The researchers told her they’d talk to
the O5 Council about it, and that they’d

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:31.600
try to convince them.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:36.430
Doctor Spiegel missed her husband, and they
were sure he felt the same about his wife.

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:42.230
Then she offered a plan - she’d walk, infinitely,
in one direction until she saw something other

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.820
than desert, then she’d call them again.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:48.519
The researchers agreed, and Doctor Spiegel
hung up the phone.

00:10:48.519 --> 00:10:51.800
Five whole days after that call, the phone
rang again.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:57.019
The researchers picked it up, only to hear
SCP-2922-B out of breath and panting.

00:10:57.019 --> 00:10:59.360
When asked what happened, she started to explain.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:03.449
She had been traveling through the desert
for five days, though this place had no day-night

00:11:03.449 --> 00:11:06.060
cycle, and she hadn’t felt tired or hungry.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:11.550
Then she encountered a mountain range, and
began to approach its base, when she saw a

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:12.570
life form.

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:14.029
But it wasn’t human.

00:11:14.029 --> 00:11:17.340
It walked on two feet and looked like a mammal,
but that was it.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:21.079
It was some kind of gigantic primate, like
a massive gorilla.

00:11:21.079 --> 00:11:25.420
It easily dwarfed the mountains she saw, and
had to have been at least 2km tall.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:31.551
She had felt it before she had seen it - huge
slow footsteps shaking the ground, then saw

00:11:31.551 --> 00:11:33.589
it exit the mountain range.

00:11:33.589 --> 00:11:38.740
She saw it had matted black fur and two huge
glowing white eyes, with no mouth.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:39.760
But then…

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:41.209
it stepped on her.

00:11:41.209 --> 00:11:43.949
Not aggressively, just out of curiosity.

00:11:43.949 --> 00:11:48.180
It seemed to be investigating her and seeing
how much force she could withstand.

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:51.910
She was crushed flat, of course, but only
a few seconds later her body put itself back

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:54.019
together and she was back to normal.

00:11:54.019 --> 00:11:57.759
Then it lost interest and went off into the
desert she had just come from.

00:11:57.759 --> 00:12:03.930
The researchers were incredulous at this seeming
confirmation that SCP-2922-C was the afterlife,

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:08.689
but they were interrupted by Doctor Spiegel
saying she could see people making campfires

00:12:08.689 --> 00:12:10.110
in a valley ahead.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:14.090
Hundreds of them, huddled up and some buried
halfway into the ground.

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:17.730
The researchers guessed they were trying to
escape their situation, and Doctor Spiegel

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:20.629
worried aloud that she’d wind up like them.

00:12:20.629 --> 00:12:24.910
Then she made another request, citing the
service she had been competently performing

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:29.670
with full loyalty: that she be allowed to
speak to her husband, but the O5s had already

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:34.139
decided she would only be authorized to speak
with other Project Corbenic personnel.

00:12:34.139 --> 00:12:38.180
She asked the Foundation to hire him, but
with an art history degree, he wouldn’t

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.250
be of use to the Foundation beyond this one
anomaly.

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:44.019
Angrily, she swore and hung up the phone.

00:12:44.019 --> 00:12:47.770
The next time they heard from her, the call
was brief and icy.

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:52.000
Doctor Spiegel was clearly still angry over
being denied the right to speak to her husband,

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.730
but claimed she had good news.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:58.500
A wagon being driven by a skeletal horse and
a tall man in a white robe had come to the

00:12:58.500 --> 00:12:59.700
human camp.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:04.470
He informed them that they were being taken
to a place called the “Elysian Fields”.

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:07.310
Remember Elysium, the Greek afterlife from
earlier?

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:11.970
Doctor Spiegel did, and believed that the
man was taking them to a sort of paradise.

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:15.920
The researchers remarked that she didn’t
seem very happy, but she quickly hung up the

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:16.940
phone.

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:22.750
After that strange exchange SCP-2922 didn’t
ring for a very long time.

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:25.649
No further transmissions came in for seven
months.

00:13:25.649 --> 00:13:30.680
All attempts to phone Doctor Spiegel instead
rang and rang, but were never picked up.

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:37.380
Project Corbenic was put on hold, until one
day a voicemail came to SCP-2922-A.

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:42.320
The voicemail was recorded by Doctor Spiegel,
but was different in tone from the other calls.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:43.910
It seemed angry.

00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:46.850
She explained that she had been keeping an
eye on the Foundation’s efforts to track

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:51.360
her down, using the fact that she had gained
the favor of some kind of powerful god-like

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:52.360
entity.

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:57.231
She had told the Foundation about the possibility
of an Elysium afterlife, a paradise, and then

00:13:57.231 --> 00:13:59.310
knew that they’d want more.

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:03.220
She had lied about the grim reaper, but everything
else had been entirely true.

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:09.160
But what happened after she visited the human
camp was, in her words, far too complicated

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:10.470
to explain.

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:13.959
But she would be more than happy to answer
the Foundation’s questions of what happens

00:14:13.959 --> 00:14:16.160
after you die, for a small price.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:21.439
If the O5 Council let her speak to her husband,
she’d tell them everything.

00:14:21.439 --> 00:14:25.720
But if they weren’t willing to accept one
civilian, then she’d know, and would never

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:27.980
tell them the real answer.

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:30.410
She signed off, ending the voicemail.

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:34.370
The researchers were shocked at this sudden
betrayal, and forwarded the request up the

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:35.370
ladder.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:41.040
But by the time the O5 Council began deliberating
on whether to involve her husband in SCP-2922,

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:42.180
something else happened.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:46.791
Foundation researchers tracked another voicemail
coming from the same kind of anomaly, except

00:14:46.791 --> 00:14:51.749
it was going to Herman Spiegel’s house,
Doctor Spiegel’s widowed husband.

00:14:51.749 --> 00:14:56.790
Figuring that she was trying to contact her
husband, Mobile Task Force Psi-8, the Silencers,

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:58.029
raided his home.

00:14:58.029 --> 00:15:02.170
But as soon as they threw open the door and
moved in, Mr. Spiegel threw the phone towards

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:05.370
the wall to destroy it, and was shot by the
soldiers.

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:09.620
The phone, however, was unharmed, and still
had one voicemail on it that the Foundation

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:11.220
was able to extract.

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:17.160
The voicemail was from SCP-2922-B, Janet Spiegel,
talking to her husband.

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:21.339
At first, she was assuring him he wasn’t
insane, and that he really was hearing his

00:15:21.339 --> 00:15:23.649
wife from beyond the grave.

00:15:23.649 --> 00:15:26.310
Then told him to listen carefully while she
explained.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:31.480
The same godlike entity she had befriended
had worked some magic to allow her to contact

00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:36.079
a phone besides SCP-2922-A one time.

00:15:36.079 --> 00:15:40.019
She told him that a Foundation task force
was headed to his house and would be there

00:15:40.019 --> 00:15:41.589
within ten minutes.

00:15:41.589 --> 00:15:46.670
Not just any task force - Psi-8, a squad dedicated
to black-ops executions.

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:50.220
There wasn’t time to run - he would die.

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:55.120
But she reassured him that all wasn’t lost,
even though the situation must be terrifying.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:59.379
She knew he was an atheist but she was proof
that there was an afterlife.

00:15:59.379 --> 00:16:04.540
And not just one - millions of afterlives,
and the one she was in was preferable to most

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:05.649
of the others.

00:16:05.649 --> 00:16:08.300
Most importantly, her husband could join her.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.890
He already knew how to get to the afterlife,
even if he didn’t remember.

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:13.559
He’d figure it out on the way.

00:16:13.559 --> 00:16:16.649
Her husband listened to her instructions with
rapt attention.

00:16:16.649 --> 00:16:20.110
She advised him on the path he should take
once he got to the desert.

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:24.320
He should follow the moon on the left until
he reached the valley where the Striders,

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:25.950
the massive primates, dwelled.

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:31.050
Then he should ask one of the trees where
she had gone, and follow that path.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:33.860
She also advised him not to do anything to
anger anyone.

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:37.140
Let the Striders kill him, it’s their way
of saying hello.

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:39.579
He’d regenerate with no harm done.

00:16:39.579 --> 00:16:43.240
But there were fates worse than death that
he should avoid at all costs.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:45.399
Getting “sent back”, for one.

00:16:45.399 --> 00:16:49.829
But before she could finish her advice, both
of them realized that the MTF were at his

00:16:49.829 --> 00:16:50.829
door.

00:16:50.829 --> 00:16:53.710
Hurriedly telling him to delete voicemail
so the Foundation wouldn’t find out, she

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:58.720
assured him that she had help from her godlike
friend and that she fully believed in him,

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:00.910
and that he should never give up.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:02.980
She told him she loved him forever.

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:10.440
By shooting Mr. Spiegel, the MTF had inadvertently
sent him to SCP-2922-C along with his wife.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:17.611
But the Foundation wasn’t done yet: SCP-2922
had let them set up Operation Galahad, a project

00:17:17.611 --> 00:17:23.270
to send trained Foundation operatives into
the afterlife to try and apprehend Janet Spiegel

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:26.670
before she could bring down the Foundation.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:30.840
Operation Galahad has plenty more stories
where that came from, but we’ll cover those

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:31.960
a different day!

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:38.930
Now go check out “SCP-2718 - What Happens
After Death” and “Project Dammerung - SCP-3448

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:44.160
- Half-Death MRI Machine” for more anomalous
afterlife action from SCP Explained!

